10.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {A. A mutation in region 1 preventing its binding to region 2}

The trp leader transcript can form alternative RNA hairpins (1-2 pause/pairing structure, 2-3 antiterminator, or 3-4 terminator). Under high tryptophan the ribosome does not stall in region 1, covers region 2 upon reaching the stop codon, and thereby favors formation of the 3-4 terminator hairpin once regions 3 and 4 are transcribed.  

A mutation that prevents region 1 from base-pairing with region 2 leaves region 2 free to pair with region 3 as soon as region 3 appears. This sequesters region 3 and blocks formation of the 3-4 terminator, allowing read-through even when tryptophan is abundant.  

The other options either strengthen termination (B, C), have no direct effect on hairpin choice (D), or simply reduce transcription initiation (E) and therefore cannot produce continued operon expression under repressing conditions.}

4.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {A. A mutation in region 1 preventing its binding to region 2}

The trp leader transcript can adopt mutually exclusive structures (1-2 pause hairpin, 2-3 antiterminator, or 3-4 terminator). Under high tryptophan the ribosome does not stall, covers region 2, and thereby favors 3-4 terminator formation. Regions 1 and 2 compete for pairing with region 2; a mutation that abolishes 1-2 pairing therefore frees region 2 to pair with region 3 even when the ribosome is not stalled. Consequently the 2-3 antiterminator forms constitutively, 3-4 cannot form, and transcription continues.  

B favors 3-4 by eliminating the antiterminator. C alters the run of U residues that follows the hairpin, not the stem-loop itself. D and E would reduce, not increase, read-through. Thus only A both blocks 3-4 formation and produces the desired high-Trp read-through phenotype.}

6.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {A}

The trp attenuator depends on mutually exclusive RNA hairpins in the trpL leader transcript. Regions 2 and 3 base-pair to form the antiterminator; regions 3 and 4 form the terminator hairpin that halts transcription when tryptophan is high.  

In high tryptophan the ribosome does not stall at the Trp codons inside region 1, allowing it to cover region 2 and thereby block formation of the 2–3 antiterminator; 3–4 is then free to form. A mutation that prevents region 1 from pairing with region 2 leaves region 2 single-stranded and therefore available to pair with region 3 even when the ribosome is not stalled. Consequently the 2–3 antiterminator forms constitutively, 3–4 cannot form, and transcription continues through the operon despite high tryptophan.  

Options B and C would favor or strengthen the terminator; D and E do not alter hairpin choice. Thus A is the mutation that blocks the 3–4 stem-loop under repressing conditions.}

