4.text:
{A. A mutation in region 1 preventing its binding to region 2}

Under high tryptophan the ribosome translates the entire leader peptide and covers region 2, leaving regions 3 and 4 free to form the terminator hairpin. A mutation that abolishes 1–2 pairing leaves region 2 single-stranded even when the ribosome is positioned over it; region 2 therefore pairs with the incoming region 3, forming the 2–3 antiterminator constitutively. This sequesters region 3 and directly blocks assembly of the 3–4 terminator, allowing read-through.  

B prevents the antiterminator and therefore favors (rather than blocks) the terminator. C strengthens the existing terminator rather than preventing its formation. D and E alter ribosome coverage or initiation frequency without changing hairpin choice. Only A constitutively selects the antiterminator under repressing conditions.

